1 DELETE
    The DELETE command removes a file from a mounted ODS-2 volume and
    reclaims its storage.

    A DELETE file specification must name a version: an explicit version
    number (;3), all versions (;*), or the newest version, written as ;0 or
    as a semicolon with no number after it (FOO.TXT;).  Unlike DIRECTORY,
    TYPE, and COPY, DELETE never defaults an omitted version to "the
    newest one"; this matches real VMS DCL's own DELETE, which requires a
    version to guard against accidentally deleting the wrong copy of a
    file.

    Several files may be deleted at once, their specifications separated
    by commas or plus signs.  Each specification after the first takes
    the device, directory, name, and type it leaves out from the one
    before it, but never the version: DELETE A.TXT;1,;2,B;1 deletes
    A.TXT;1, A.TXT;2, and B.TXT;1.  A specification that fails is
    reported, and the rest are still deleted.

    As VMS's DELETE, it says nothing of the files it deletes unless /LOG is
    given.  A specification that finds no file is a warning:

        %DELETE-W-SEARCHFAIL, error searching for DUA0:[WORK]X.TMP;*
        -RMS-E-FNF, file not found

    Syntax:

        DELETE[/LOG] <file-spec>;<version>[,...]

    Parameter:

        <file-spec>;<version>[,...]
                        The files, and versions, to delete.  <file-spec>
                        may include wildcards in its name/type; any part
                        left out of the first is filled in from the current
                        default device/directory (see SET DEFAULT), and of
                        the others from the specification before it.

    Qualifier:

        /LOG            Show each file deleted, and the blocks it had:
                        %DELETE-I-FILDEL, DUA0:[WORK]FOO.TXT;1 deleted
                        (3 blocks)

    Examples:

        DELETE FOO.TXT;1

        DELETE FOO.TXT;

        DELETE OLDFILE.DAT;*

        DELETE A.LIS;*,B;*,C.OBJ;*

        DEL *.TMP;1

    See also the help for PURGE, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.
2 /SYMBOL
    The DELETE/SYMBOL command deletes a symbol (see HELP SYMBOL).

    Syntax:

        DELETE/SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] symbol-name
        DELETE/SYMBOL [/LOCAL | /GLOBAL] /ALL

    The name may be any abbreviation the symbol allows.

    Qualifiers:

        /LOCAL          Delete from the local symbol table of the current
                        command level (the default).
        /GLOBAL         Delete from the global symbol table.
        /ALL            Delete every symbol in the table, instead of one.
